    {
        "slug": "chicken-stock-vs-broth-what-s-the-difference",
        "template_type": "comparison",
        "title": "Chicken Stock vs. Broth: What's the Difference?",
        "batch_number": 1,
        "content": {
            "meta_description": "Chicken stock vs. broth: the real difference comes down to bones versus meat, and the body it gives your dish. Here's when to use each.",
            "hero_image_query": "chicken stock and chicken broth side by side in glass jars",
            "image_alt": "Two glass jars side by side, one holding pale golden chicken stock with a gelatinous wobble, the other holding thinner, clearer chicken broth, with a raw chicken carcass and fresh herbs nearby.",
            "item_a_name": "Chicken Stock",
            "item_b_name": "Chicken Broth",
            "comparison_table": [
                {
                    "attribute": "Main ingredient",
                    "item_a": "Chicken bones, often roasted, plus scraps like necks and feet",
                    "item_b": "Chicken meat, sometimes with some bones included",
                },
                {
                    "attribute": "Cook time",
                    "item_a": "4-8 hours to extract collagen",
                    "item_b": "1-2 hours, just long enough to flavor the water",
                },
                {
                    "attribute": "Texture when chilled",
                    "item_a": "Gels or thickens due to dissolved gelatin",
                    "item_b": "Stays liquid, no gel",
                },
                {
                    "attribute": "Seasoning",
                    "item_a": "Usually unsalted or lightly salted",
                    "item_b": "Often salted and ready to sip on its own",
                },
                {
                    "attribute": "Flavor",
                    "item_a": "Deep, rounder, more body from collagen",
                    "item_b": "Lighter, more straightforwardly chicken-flavored",
                },
                {
                    "attribute": "Best uses",
                    "item_a": "Sauces, gravies, risottos, braises where body matters",
                    "item_b": "Soups meant to be eaten as-is, quick pan sauces, poaching",
                },
            ],
            "verdict": "Reach for chicken stock when a recipe needs body and richness, like gravy, risotto, or a braise. Reach for chicken broth when you want a lighter, ready-to-eat liquid, like a quick soup or a poaching liquid, or when you're short on time.",
            "sections": [
                {
                    "heading": "Chicken Stock",
                    "body": "Made by simmering chicken bones, often roasted first for deeper color and flavor, along with aromatics like onion, carrot, and celery, for anywhere from 4 to 8 hours. The long cook time pulls collagen out of the bones and connective tissue, which dissolves into gelatin. That's why a good stock turns jiggly and semi-solid once refrigerated. It's typically left unsalted so it can be reduced or used as a base without over-salting the final dish. The gelatin content is also what gives sauces and gravies made from stock their glossy, thick mouthfeel.",
                },
                {
                    "heading": "Chicken Broth",
                    "body": "Made by simmering chicken meat, and sometimes a few bones, for a shorter 1 to 2 hours. Because there's less time and often less bone content, less collagen breaks down, so broth stays thin and liquid even when cold. It's usually seasoned with salt during cooking since it's meant to be flavorful enough to drink or use straight in a soup without much doctoring. The flavor leans lighter and more straightforwardly savory, since it's driven by meat rather than bone marrow and connective tissue.",
                },
                {
                    "heading": "Can you substitute one for the other?",
                    "body": "In most recipes, yes, especially in soups, sauces, and braises where the liquid gets reduced or combined with other ingredients. If a recipe calls for stock and you only have broth, taste and adjust salt since broth is usually already seasoned, and expect a slightly thinner sauce since you'll be missing that gelatin body. If a recipe calls for broth and you only have stock, add salt to taste and thin it slightly with water if the dish needs a lighter consistency.",
                },
            ],
            "faqs": [
                {
                    "question": "Why does my homemade chicken stock turn into jelly in the fridge?",
                    "answer": "That's dissolved collagen from the bones and connective tissue setting up as gelatin once it cools, and it's a sign of a well-made, collagen-rich stock. It melts back into liquid as soon as it's reheated.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "Is bone broth the same as chicken stock?",
                    "answer": "They're nearly identical in method, both are made by simmering bones for hours to extract gelatin. Bone broth is usually simmered even longer, sometimes 12-24 hours, and is often marketed and lightly seasoned to be sipped on its own.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "Which one is better for making gravy?",
                    "answer": "Chicken stock, because the gelatin it contains helps the gravy thicken naturally and gives it a silkier, more clingy texture on food.",
                },
                {
                    "question": "Can I use store-bought broth to make something that needs stock's richness?",
                    "answer": "Simmer the broth down by a third to concentrate the flavor, and stir in a small spoonful of unflavored gelatin to mimic the body a real stock would provide.",
                },
            ],
            "item_a_link": None,
            "item_b_link": None,
        },
    },
